# The gate

`scripts/gate.sh` is the only gate of this repo. The template owns this file
and the gate: do not edit either one here. `copier update` keeps both current.
Change them in the project template (komputa-practice).

## Commands

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `scripts/gate.sh --fast` | On commit (the pre-commit hook): format, lint and secret scan on staged files, about 5 s. It refuses a staged file that has more changes on disk |
| `scripts/gate.sh` | The default while you build: the full checks of only the sections that the diff since the merge base touches (docs alone run none). A path that maps to no section, no merge base or no change runs every section; the gate says which on one line. Skips a clean tree that already passed |
| `scripts/gate.sh --full` | Before a PR and in CI: every section in full (format, lint, type check, every test), and a secret scan of the files the branch changed (every tracked file when there is no merge base); a key committed with `--no-verify` fails CI. A Python, .NET or TypeScript project also gets the dependency scan (below). Must pass before a PR |
| `scripts/gate.sh --only SECTION` | One section only, for example `--only shell`. CI runs one per image. In CI a section whose files the change does not touch is skipped (the diff against the target branch, or the first parent of a merge preview); shell and local always run, because the secret scan is in shell |

- Section budgets: each section writes its time to `.gate/` and fails the gate
  over its budget: fast {{ gate_fast_budget }} s{% if gate_full_budget %}, full {{ gate_full_budget }} s{% endif %}. The failure names the slowest
  tests or steps. Raise a budget only with `gate_budget_reason` in
  `.copier-answers.yml`.
{%- if 'python' in languages or 'dotnet' in languages or 'typescript' in languages %}
- Dependency scan: a full run (CI) scans every `uv.lock`, `package-lock.json` and
  `packages.lock.json` that git tracks, for known vulnerabilities, with Trivy
  (`scripts/install-trivy.sh` installs the release pinned in `scripts/trivy.pin`,
  checked by sha256). It fails on HIGH and CRITICAL findings that have a fix, and
  names each package and CVE. Fix it by upgrading the package. To accept a finding,
  add it to `.trivyignore.yaml` with a reason; the gate refuses an entry without one:

  ```yaml
  vulnerabilities:
    - id: CVE-2026-0001
      statement: The vulnerable function is never called here.
      expired_at: 2027-03-01 # optional: the acceptance lapses on this date
  ```

  `--fast` does not run it: the Trivy binary and its vulnerability database take
  longer than the 5 s budget, and CI scans the same files. A lock file change also
  runs the shell section in the bare command, where the scan lives. Trivy downloads
  its database on each run (cached in `.gate/trivy`), so the scan needs the network;
  offline it fails, unless an earlier run left a database and you set
  `TRIVY_SKIP_DB_UPDATE=true`. Rust keeps `cargo deny`. Development dependencies are
  not scanned. The weekly dependency job moves the pin to a release at least seven
  days old, after it checks the release's cosign signature.
{%- endif %}
- Test lines: a full run prints the source lines and test lines the branch adds
  against its merge base. It never fails on them. How many tests a PR needs
  follows risk: see "Tests follow risk" in `komputa-practice`
  (`rules/working-rules.md`).
- File size: `--fast` allows a staged source file over 5,000 tokens (bytes / 4), and
  asks for a refactor issue on the forge at high priority (OneDev
  `Priority=Major`, GitHub label `priority: high`), unless one is open. A large
  file costs tokens on every later call of an agent that read it. The refactor
  splits it: one module for each concern, and tests in their own file. Only
  source code counts (by extension, or a `#!` script): documents such as HTML,
  Markdown and CSS never do, nor lock files, generated code, test data and
  binary files.
  `.gate-size-baseline` lists the files over the cap ("<tokens> <path>" on each
  line); the gate adds a file at its new size, so it asks again only when the
  file grows. `scripts/gate.sh --size-baseline` writes the list.
- Branch name: every branch is `<kind>/<name>`. The kinds are `feat fix docs refactor chore perf
  test ci prune`; the name is lower-case words joined by `-`, with the issue number first when there
  is one (`fix/312-spec-drift`). `--fast` refuses a commit on a branch named otherwise, and CI refuses
  the pull request where the forge names its branch (GitHub's `GITHUB_HEAD_REF`; set `GATE_BRANCH` for
  another forge). The default branch (`{{ default_branch }}`), `main`, `master`, bot branches (`dependabot/`, `renovate/`) and a detached checkout
  pass. Rename a branch with `git branch -m <kind>/<name>`.
- No hook runs on push. CI runs the full gate on the pull request.
- The build spec is seeded once and then belongs to the repo, so `copier update`
  never changes it. `scripts/gate.sh --full` checks it for the lines that the
  template's gate depends on (the Deslop, secret scan and dependency jobs, the target-branch
  fetch and `CI=true` in the gate steps, and the trust jobs when `trust_green_ci`
  is on) and names each one that is missing. Copy the line from the template.

## The nightly secret scan

- The job "Secret scan nightly" (`.github/workflows/trufflehog-nightly.yml` on GitHub) runs at
  the same time as the Deslop nightly. It scans the whole history of `{{ default_branch }}` with
  TruffleHog (`scripts/trufflehog-scan.sh`) and keeps only secrets that are verified live.
- TruffleHog verifies a secret by calling the provider that issued it, so each candidate
  leaves the CI runner for that provider. The job needs outbound internet.
- Each live secret becomes one high-priority issue (`scripts/trufflehog-issues.sh`): GitHub
  label `priority: high`, which must exist in the repo, or OneDev `Priority=Major`. A
  fingerprint of detector, commit, file and line stops a second issue, open or closed. At most
  10 issues a night. The issue says where the secret is and never holds the value; neither does
  the job log. Rotate the credential first. Rewriting history is optional once it is revoked.
- The image is pinned by tag and digest in the job. The weekly "Update dependencies" job moves
  the digest with the other image pins, and only to an image seven days old or more.
- On OneDev the job reads the secret `ONEDEV_ISSUES_TOKEN`, as the Deslop nightly does.

## The repo's own checks

- Do not add another gate script.
- Put the repo's own checks in `scripts/gate.local.sh`. The repo owns that file.
  The gate runs it from the repo root with one argument: `full` or `fast`.
  In fast mode, check staged files only and stay inside the 5 s budget.
  For example:

  ```bash
  #!/usr/bin/env bash
  set -Eeuo pipefail
  bad=$(gofmt -l .)
  [[ -z "$bad" ]] || { printf 'gofmt: %s\n' "$bad" >&2; exit 1; }
  if [[ "$1" == full ]]; then
    go vet ./...
    go test ./...
  fi
  ```
- An old gate script (for example `gate.sh`, `check.sh`, `ci.sh` or
  `quality.sh`, in any folder) is folded into `scripts/gate.local.sh` and
  then removed, together with the CI steps that call it. The PR that removes it
  lists where each of its checks went. The gate fails while one is tracked
  outside a test-data folder (`fixtures`, `__fixtures__`, `tests`, `test`,
  `spec` or `testdata`); a vendored folder goes in the `shell_exclude` answer
  instead. The two exemptions are `scripts/gate.sh` and its PowerShell twin
  `scripts/gate.ps1`.
